# QHV (QNX Hypervisor) hosting a QNX guest — QEMU-TCG, aarch64
#
# Captured: 2026-06-11, local Windows build host (qemu-system-aarch64 11.0, TCG).
# Build: SDP 8.0.4 mkqnximage.
#   guest : mkqnximage --type=qvm   --arch=aarch64le --build
#   host  : mkqnximage --type=qemu  --arch=aarch64le --qvm=yes --guest=<guest-dir> --build
# Launch (no KVM — pure TCG emulation of an EL2-capable CPU so QHV's el2-host/VHE comes up):
#   qemu-system-aarch64 -machine virt,virtualization=on,gic-version=3 -cpu max -accel tcg \
#       -smp 2 -m 2G -drive file=disk-qemu,if=none,id=drv0,format=raw \
#       -device virtio-blk-device,drive=drv0 -kernel ifs.bin -nographic
#
# The guest is auto-started from the host's post_startup.sh via a custom snippet
# (qhv/host/local/snippets/post_start.custom) that builds a no-network qvm config
# and runs `qvm @g2.conf`. Networking was dropped because the host io-sock stack
# does not initialise on this qemu-virt build (see the "network stack down" lines).
#
# LOAD-BEARING EVIDENCE — two distinct QNX instances with different machines.
#   CORRECTION (2026-09-08/09): the HOST prints NO banner; an earlier version of
#   this header said to look for "QNX qnx-qhv ... QEMU_virt" and no such line
#   exists in the body below. The host is evidenced by "=== AUTO-START QNX
#   GUEST UNDER QVM" (it reached post_start) and "=== launching qvm @g2.conf".
#   GUEST banner: "QNX qnx-guest 8.0.0 ... ARMv8_Foundation_Model aarch64le"
# The guest sees ARMv8_Foundation_Model — the virtual platform QHV synthesises —
# NOT the underlying QEMU_virt machine. That is the Type-1 partition boundary.
#
# HONEST FRAMING: TCG proves the QHV *software* architecture (qvm config parsing,
# vdev instantiation, guest isolation, EL2/VHE host) — it does NOT prove hardware
# timing or acceleration (that needs c7g.metal or real Orin silicon). AWS non-metal
# Graviton exposes no /dev/kvm / EL2, so this could not be KVM-accelerated there.
# =====================================================================================

